{"turn": 8, "tool": "submit_findings", "args": {"findings": [{"provider_id": "1000000033", "leaning": "pattern_consistent_with_upcoding", "headline": "Bills 99215 on 27.6% of visits against 2.2% expected, mostly for healthy patients. 11 of 30 random charts support a lower level than billed.", "interpretation": "This was the top risk-adjusted outlier (z_adj 31). The 99215 rate is 22.5% for patients with no chronic conditions (peers 2.4%) and 28.3% for patients with one (peers 3.6%). We received a random sample of 30 charts, all usable. 11 of 30 (36.7%) support a lower level than billed, and 7 of the 8 documented 99215 claims (88%) fall below the billed level, against a peer rate of 3%. The draft overpayment estimate from the random sample is $22,043 (CI $16,065 to $28,022). This needs human review of the charts before any recovery."}, {"provider_id": "1000000075", "leaning": "pattern_consistent_with_upcoding", "headline": "Bills 99215 on 25.9% of visits against 3.2% expected, including 20.6% for patients with no conditions. Notes describe data review that the claims don't show.", "interpretation": "Claims: 47.6% of this provider's 99215s go to patients with 0 or 1 conditions, often for back pain, URI, rash or abdominal pain. Charts (random sample of 30): only 1 of 11 documented 99215 claims is below the billed level, so the charts mostly support the bills. But in 7 of the 25 notes that claim moderate-or-higher data review, the claim shows no lab work, and most 99215 notes have no time recorded. The chart support may be inflated to match the bill, so it counts for little. The leaning rests mainly on the claims pattern. It is weaker than for 033, and I made no overpayment estimate because charts rarely showed a lower level. A human should check the notes against the claims before acting."}, {"provider_id": "1000000128", "leaning": "inconclusive", "headline": "Bills 99215 on 22.8% of visits against 1.9% expected, and 91% of those are for low-complexity patients. The charts largely support the levels billed.", "interpretation": "The claims pattern is a strong concern: 19.9% 99215 for patients with no conditions (peers 2.4%). But in a random sample of 30 charts, all 13 documented 99215 claims were supported, with high MDM and 40 to 49 minutes recorded. Only 1 chart (a 99214) was below billed. In 5 of 22 notes, data review isn't matched by labs on the claim, which is the same rate as the sicker-panel provider 1000000110, so it doesn't stand out. Possibly time-based billing. Worth a human look; not referred for recovery."}, {"provider_id": "1000000096", "leaning": "inconclusive", "headline": "Bills 99215 on 23.5% of visits against 4.7% expected, high at every complexity level. 0 of 30 random charts were below the billed level.", "interpretation": "The panel is moderately sick, and the 99215 rate is elevated at every complexity level. The random charts fully support the billing, with 2 of 30 notes showing data review the claims don't show. The claims and charts point in different directions, so this stays inconclusive."}, {"provider_id": "1000000110", "leaning": "pattern_consistent_with_high_acuity_panel", "headline": "54.7% of visits billed as 99215, but 557 of 758 visits are for patients with 5 or more conditions. Healthy patients get almost no 99215s.", "interpretation": "The panel is very sick (expected share 14.3%). For patients with no conditions, the 99215 rate is 0%. Only 2 of 30 random charts were below billed. The 99215 rate for the sickest patients is still above peers (65.7% vs 27.5%), so this deserves light monitoring. Overall it looks like a high-acuity panel."}, {"provider_id": "1000000045", "leaning": "pattern_consistent_with_high_acuity_panel", "headline": "99215 share is 14.6% against 4.9% expected. The excess sits in sicker patients, and 1 of 30 random charts was below billed.", "interpretation": "For patients with no conditions, the 99215 rate is 2.7%, about the same as peers (2.4%). The rate climbs with complexity. The charts support the billing, and only 1 note showed data review the claims don't show."}, {"provider_id": "1000000013", "leaning": "inconclusive", "headline": "Bills 99215 on 15.9% of visits against 3.4% expected (z_adj 10.9). No charts received.", "interpretation": "The office objected to the scope of the request, then didn't respond to the follow-up. Records closed as non-responsive. Non-response is not evidence of upcoding. The claims pattern alone (9.4% 99215 for patients with no conditions vs 2.4% for peers) supports another look later, but not a conclusion now."}, {"provider_id": "1000000216", "leaning": "inconclusive", "headline": "Bills 99215 on 10.9% of visits against 2.0% expected (z_adj 7.8). No charts received.", "interpretation": "The office asked for an extension, then didn't respond. Records closed as non-responsive, which is not evidence. The excess is modest and partly in more complex patients. No conclusion possible."}]}, "result_chars": 94, "result": "{\"accepted\": 8, \"status\": \"drafts for human review; nothing is sent or decided automatically\"}", "usd_so_far": 0.2367}
